  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 126 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A weak shortwave will pass through the region from \nnorthwest to southeast this afternoon and evening, but the strongest \ndynamics will mostly be to the south. An associated area of showers \nand embedded thunderstorms has developed west of Ottawa and those \nwill move southeast into southwestern St. Lawrence County. A briefly \nstronger storm is possible there. The storms should mostly pass \nsouth of Rutland County, though a brief isolated shower is possible \nthere. Pea sized hail and gusty winds are possible in the strongest \nstorm in St. Lawrence County. After that, a gorgeous opener to the \nweekend will occur on Saturday with seasonably cool temperatures and \nlow humidity.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A double barrel low will move into the region late Sunday \ninto Sunday night, bringing rounds of rain. The primary low will \ntrack well north of the St. Lawrence, while the secondary low will \ntry to spin up off the coast of southern New England, but they will \nboth remain on the weaker side. The Euro ensembles generally bring \nthe heavier rain associated with the southern low farther north and \ninto the region, while the GEFS members generally split the region \nbetween the areas of low pressure and bring lighter precip. Overall, \ncombined EPS/GEFS/CMCE probabilities of receiving more than a half \ninch are between 40-70 percent, and the probabilities of seeing more \nthan an inch are less than 10 percent. Some convective enhancement \nis possible across southern areas, but no flooding is expected. Very \nweak lapse rates, a notable inversion and unfavorable timing should \nprevent much of a severe threat, though a trend north in storm track \ncould still produce something, so the threat will continue to be \nwatched. A few lingering showers are possible on Monday as a weak \nshortwave moves through under broad cyclonic flow.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: High pressure and calm weather embedded in broad \ncyclonic flow will continue through Tuesday as temperatures slowly \nrise to near or just above seasonable levels. Highs will be in the \nupper 70s to low 80s heading into the mid week. Humidity will remain \non the lower side Tuesday, but as flow shift slightly to the \nsouthwest by late Tuesday into Wednesday, some moisture advection is \nexpected by mid week. The next weather maker will arrive by \nWednesday into Thursday with a cold frontal passage. Models are in \npretty good agreement for rain and possible thunder chances \nWednesday afternoon for the higher terrain and into late Wednesday \nfor most locations, however, how long showers linger remains in \nquestion. The GFS is the fastest of the model suite clearing the \narea of precipitation by Thursday afternoon. The Euro and Canadian \nfavor a longer system, with more positive tilting to the trough \naxis, and precipitation persisting through the day Thursday. \nRegardless, pwats look to increase to around an inch, though where \nthe best forcing sets up will determine how much of the precipitable \nwater is realized. Temperatures mid to late week will slowly \ndecrease from the low 80s to the upper 70s, along with a drying \nsurface with dewpoints sinking back down to the low 50s.",
